<article class="env-corollary" id="06QN">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06QN">Corollary <span data-tag="06QN">3.5.9.26</span>.</a> Let $n$ be an integer, and suppose we are given an inverse system of Kan complexes </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \cdots \rightarrow X(3) \rightarrow X(2) \rightarrow X(1) \rightarrow X(0) \]
</div>
<p> where the transition maps are Kan fibrations. If each of the Kan complexes $X(k)$ is $n$-truncated, then the limit $\varprojlim X(k)$ is also $n$-truncated. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      It follows from Proposition <a data-tag="056C" href="/tag/056C">3.5.9.13</a> that each of the transition maps $X(k+1) \rightarrow X(k)$ is $n$-truncated. Applying Corollary <a data-tag="06QM" href="/tag/06QM">3.5.9.25</a>, we see that the map $\varprojlim X(k) \rightarrow X(0)$ is $n$-truncated. Since $X(0)$ is an $n$-truncated Kan complex, Proposition <a data-tag="056C" href="/tag/056C">3.5.9.13</a> guarantees that $\varprojlim X(k)$ is also an $n$-truncated Kan complex. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</article>
